  "result": "Fixed. `IntRange` with `clamp=True` and an open bound now clamps to a value inside the range.\n\n**Cause:** `IntRange._clamp` in `src/click/types.py` returned `bound - dir` when the bound was open. The direction is the step *into* the range, so the sign was backwards. For example, `IntRange(0, 5, min_open=True, clamp=True)` turned `-1` into `-1`, and `IntRange(0, 5, max_open=True, clamp=True)` turned `10` into `6`. Both results are outside the range.\n\n**Change:**\n- `src/click/types.py`: `return bound - dir` \u2192 `return bound + dir`.\n- `tests/test_types.py`: added four clamp cases covering both open bounds, including the boundary value itself (e.g. `\"0\"` with `min_open` clamps to `1`).\n\n**Verification:** the full suite passes against the local `src` (1910 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed). I didn't run the new cases against the unfixed code to confirm they fail there. The arithmetic is simple enough that I'm confident they would.\n\n`FloatRange` is unaffected. It rejects `clamp` with open bounds at construction time, so it never reaches this code path.\n\nI did not commit anything.",
